    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "# Explore Pie Charts\r\n\r\nInvestigate how grouping changes what a pie chart shows.\r\n\r\n1. Run the program and compare the printed counts with the chart slices.\r\n2. Ask AI Tutor to explain how `groupby('Creature').size()` works and what each number represents.\r\n3. Change the grouping from `Creature` to `Region`. Rename the variable and update the chart labels and title.\r\n4. Run the program. Check that the chart contains one slice for each region.\r\n\r\n:::: details [Need Help?]\r\n\r\nAsk AI Tutor to explain `groupby()` or `size()` using the current dataset. Make and test the changes yourself.\r\n\r\n::::",
    "ai_tutor_available": "false",
    "submittable": "false",
    "hide_share_and_remix": "false",
    "predict_settings": {
      "isPredictLevel": false
    },
    "start_sources": {
      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "language": "py",
          "contents": "import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Read the dataset\ndata = pd.read_csv('Urban_Mythical_Creature_Sightings.csv')\n\n# Group the data by Creature and count the rows in each group\ncreature_counts = data.groupby('Creature').size()\nprint(creature_counts)\n\n# Create a pie chart of the sighting records\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 6))\nplt.pie(\n    creature_counts,\n    labels=creature_counts.index,\n    autopct='%1.1f%%',\n    startangle=140\n)\nplt.title('Distribution of Sighting Records by Creature')\n\n# Display the chart\nplt.show()",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true,
          "open": true
        },
